Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.


1. SRP Violation — Wrong Design

Instead of separating reading and writing, imagine we combine both responsibilities into one class.

// Java
public class FileUtil {

    // Responsibility 1 → Reading file
    public String readFile(String filePath) {
        // Logic to read file
        return "file content";
    }

    // Responsibility 2 → Writing file
    public void writeFile(String filePath, String content) {
        // Logic to write file
    }
}

// Java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        FileUtil fileUtil = new FileUtil();

        String content = fileUtil.readFile("input.txt");
        fileUtil.writeFile("output.txt", content);
    }
}

Why this violates SRP

FileUtil has multiple reasons to change:
1️⃣ Read logic changes
If file reading changes (new format, encoding, buffering, compression), this class changes.

2️⃣ Write logic changes
If writing logic changes (append mode, encryption, permissions), the same class changes again.

➡ One class handling multiple responsibilities = SRP violation

2. Fix:


Example: Separate file reading and file writing responsibilities into different classes.

// Java
public class FileReaderUtil {

    public String readFile(String filePath) {
        // Logic to read file and return content
        return "file content";
    }
}


// Java
public class FileWriterUtil {

    public void writeFile(String filePath, String content) {
        // Logic to write content to file
    }
}


// Java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        FileReaderUtil reader = new FileReaderUtil();
        String content = reader.readFile("input.txt");

        FileWriterUtil writer = new FileWriterUtil();
        writer.writeFile("output.txt", content);
    }
}

Summary:
Each class has a single responsibility:
FileReaderUtil only reads files
FileWriterUtil only writes files
This makes the code easier to maintain and extend, following SRP.
